---

## 🔬 Test Smell Audit — testsmells.org 19-Smell Catalog

**Project:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests` (MSTest v4, .NET 10)
**Integration-test calibration:** Applied. Class is tagged `[TestCategory("Integration")]` and project name ends in `.IntegrationTests`. Mystery Guest and Eager Test severity are downgraded per calibration rules.

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 1           | 4              |
| **Total**| **5**       | **8** (of 8)   |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — 1. Sleepy Test

**Formal taxonomy name:** *Sleepy Test*
**Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)
**Calibration note:** Even in integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` is a smell — it introduces non-determinism and slows the suite unconditionally.

```csharp
// SMELL: blocks thread for 3 seconds regardless of actual timing
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Fix:**
```csharp
// Poll with timeout — fast when condition is met, fails deterministically
var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    if (sw.Elapsed > TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5))
        Assert.Fail("Notification not sent within timeout.");
    await Task.Delay(50);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures when CI is slow; 3 s wasted on every run even when event fires in 10 ms.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — 2. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Formal taxonomy name:** *Unknown Test / Empty Test*
**Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
// SMELL: no assertions — will pass even if Insert silently corrupts data
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
{
    _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
```

**Fix:**
```csharp
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

var all = _repository.ListAll();
Assert.AreEqual(100, all.Count);
Assert.IsTrue(all.All(u => u.Id > 0), "Every inserted user should have an assigned ID.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100 % coverage on bulk path with zero defect detection.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — 3. Conditional Test Logic

**Formal taxonomy name:** *Conditional Test Logic*
**Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)

```csharp
// SMELL: only one branch executes per run — the other is never verified
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix:** Split into two focused tests or assert the deterministic type:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_ReturnsStandardUser_WithCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<User>(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden gap — if the `PremiumUser` branch is the expected path, the `else` silently covers a bug.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — 4. Assertion Roulette

**Formal taxonomy name:** *Assertion Roulette*
**Methods:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28), `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` (line 122) — 4 message-less assertions in the first.

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
// ↑ If any of these fail, MSTest reports "Assert.AreEqual failed" with no context
```

**Fix:** Add failure messages:
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should be persisted");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should be persisted");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated ID should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slow diagnosis — CI failure log says only "expected True, got False" with no clue which property was wrong.

---

#### 🟢 LOW — 5. General Fixture (Over-broad Setup)

**Formal taxonomy name:** *General Fixture*
**Scope:** `[TestInitialize] Setup()` (line 12)

`_repository.InitializeSchema()` is called before every test, but tests like `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` and `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` don't depend on a clean schema — the in-memory SQLite resets naturally. Similarly, `_connection` and `_repository` are both initialized even when a test only uses one.

**Calibration:** Flagged as Low because every test *does* use `_repository`, and the in-memory database makes this cheap. This is borderline and acceptable for integration tests.

**Risk if unfixed:** Minimal — slight cognitive overhead, negligible performance cost with SQLite `:memory:`.

---

### ✅ Smell-Free Patterns (Well-Designed Tests)

The following tests are clean, well-structured integration tests:

| Test | Notes |
|------|-------|
| `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` | Clear Arrange/Act/Assert, targeted assertions (Roulette is minor) |
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Focused on one mutation, verifies result |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single behavior, negative assertion |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion contextually clear (3 items added → assert 3) |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts` | Real concurrency scenario with deterministic assertion |

---

### Smells NOT Found (Cleared)

| Smell | Status |
|-------|--------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Not present — uses in-memory SQLite (acceptable for integration) |
| Eager Test | ✅ Not present — each test focuses on one operation (calibrated for integration) |
| Magic Number Test | ✅ Not present — all numbers are contextually obvious (3 inserts → assert 3) |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Not present — no `.ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ Not present — no try/catch in tests |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Not present |
| Duplicate Assert | ✅ Not present |
| Lazy Test | ✅ Not present |
| Redundant Print | ✅ Not present |
| Redundant Assertion | ✅ Not present |
| Default Test | ✅ Not present |
| Constructor Initialization | ✅ Not present — uses `[TestInitialize]` correctly |
| Resource Optimism | ✅ Not present |
| Empty Test | ✅ Not present |

---

### 📋 Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Effort | Impact |
|----------|-------|--------|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** — `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` | 5 min | Eliminates false-pass risk |
| 2 | **Sleepy Test** — `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` | 10 min | Eliminates flake + saves 3 s/run |
| 3 | **Conditional Test Logic** — `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` | 10 min | Removes hidden gap |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** — add messages to multi-assert tests | 5 min | Faster failure diagnosis |
| 5 | **General Fixture** — no action needed | — | Acceptable for integration tests |

**Bottom line:** 3 of 8 tests have material smells. The suite's biggest risks are the assertion-free bulk test (false confidence) and the `Thread.Sleep` (flakiness). Fix those two and the suite goes from "debatable" to "solid."
